text:
After abolishing privileges, the deputies want to write down the principles of the new society. Even before the constitution, they write a declaration of rights, as the Americans had done.

On 26 August 1789, the Assembly adopts the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen. It has 17 articles. The first one is the most famous:

"Men are born and remain free and equal in rights."

This is a revolutionary idea! Before, your rights depended on your birth. From now on, they are the same for everyone.

Here are some of the rights it proclaims:
- liberty, property, safety and resistance to oppression are natural rights;
- [sovereignty] belongs to the nation, that is, to the people, and not to the king;
- the law is the same for everyone;
- nobody can be arrested except for reasons set out in the law;
- everyone is presumed innocent until they have been found guilty;
- everyone has the right to their opinions, even religious ones;
- everyone can speak, write and print freely;
- taxes must be shared according to what each person can afford.

The text is placed "under the auspices of the Supreme Being". It is often shown as two stone tablets, like the tablets of the Law, with the eye of reason above them.

But the Declaration has limits: it says nothing about women, who will not get political rights. And it does not end slavery in the colonies.

Even so, this text changed the world. It inspired many countries and the Universal Declaration of Human Rights of 1948. It is still part of the French Constitution today!

Before the Revolution, each region had its own measurements. The foot, the ell, the toise, the league, the bushel... changed from one town to another! Merchants could easily cheat, and the books of grievances often asked for "one king, one law, one weight, one measure".

The revolutionaries decide to create a single system of measurement, simple and equal for everyone, that the whole world could use. They say it will be "for all times, for all peoples".

Scientists like Condorcet, Lagrange and Laplace suggest choosing a measure taken from nature: the Earth itself! The [metre] will be equal to one ten-millionth of the distance between the North Pole and the equator.

To work it out, two astronomers, Delambre and Méchain, measure the distance between Dunkirk and Barcelona very precisely, aiming their instruments at church towers and mountaintops. Their mission lasts from 1792 to 1799, right in the middle of the Revolution and the war! They are sometimes arrested, because people think they are spies.

On 7 April 1795 (18 Germinal Year III), a law officially creates the [metric system]. Everything is based on the number 10, which makes calculations easy: it is a [decimal] system.

- the metre for length;
- the gram for mass (the weight of one cubic centimetre of water);
- the litre for volume;
- the are for area;
- and the franc, a new currency divided into 100 centimes.

In 1799, a standard metre bar made of platinum is produced and kept in the Archives.

At first, the French find it hard to give up their old habits. But today, almost every country in the world uses the metric system. It is one of the finest legacies of the Revolution!
